1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,621
This is STAT 331, video 15. The topic is the distribution of sigma squared hat in multiple linear regression.

2
00:00:10,071 --> 00:00:33,389
After this video, you can explain why the t test needs a chi square result and an independence result. You can follow each step of the proof. You can show that the eigenvalues of an idempotent matrix are 0 or 1, and find the trace of I minus H. And you can explain why sigma squared hat divides by n minus p plus 1.

3
00:00:33,839 --> 00:00:52,015
Recall simple linear regression. Beta 1 hat minus beta 1, over sigma over root S x x, is standard normal. But sigma is unknown. So we replace it by sigma hat. We used three facts. Fact 1: Z is standard normal. We showed this.

4
00:00:52,465 --> 00:01:03,793
Fact 2: V, the sum of squared residuals over sigma squared, has a chi square distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. We only claimed this.

5
00:01:04,243 --> 00:01:08,787
Fact 3: Z and V are independent. We also only claimed this.

6
00:01:09,237 --> 00:01:19,882
With these three facts, Z over the square root of V over its degrees of freedom has a t distribution. This is the definition of the t distribution.

7
00:01:20,332 --> 00:01:34,305
The sigmas cancel. What is left is beta 1 hat minus beta 1, over sigma hat over root S x x. So this statistic has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

8
00:01:34,755 --> 00:01:51,630
In multiple linear regression, the same argument works. From video 14, beta j hat is normal with mean beta j and variance sigma squared V j j. So fact 1 holds: the standardized beta j hat is standard normal.

9
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:05,883
We still need fact 2: e transpose e over sigma squared has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. And fact 3: beta hat and e are independent.

10
00:02:06,333 --> 00:02:22,183
Then, by the same argument, beta j hat minus beta j, over the square root of sigma squared hat V j j, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Now we prove facts 2 and 3.

11
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:39,145
Why does this matter? The t distribution has heavier tails than the normal, when the degrees of freedom are small. For data set 1 there are only 2 degrees of freedom. The critical value is 4.303, not 1.96.

12
00:02:39,595 --> 00:02:51,563
Fact 3 is short. In video 14 b, we found the joint distribution of beta hat and e. It is multivariate normal, and the covariance blocks are zero.

13
00:02:52,013 --> 00:02:59,608
For the multivariate normal, zero covariance means independence. So e is independent of beta hat.

14
00:03:00,058 --> 00:03:14,927
e transpose e over sigma squared is a function of e only. A function of e is independent of beta hat too. So sigma squared hat is independent of every beta j hat. Fact 3 is done.

15
00:03:15,377 --> 00:03:36,327
Now fact 2. We want: e transpose e over sigma squared has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Recall the definition from Lecture 1. If Z 1 to Z nu are independent standard normal, the sum of their squares is chi square with nu degrees of freedom.

16
00:03:36,777 --> 00:03:48,339
The picture shows the chi square density for 1, 2 and 5 terms. Each extra squared term moves the density to the right. The degrees of freedom count the terms.

17
00:03:48,789 --> 00:03:58,987
So the plan is: write e transpose e over sigma squared as a sum of squared independent standard normal variables. Then count the terms.

18
00:03:59,437 --> 00:04:07,501
A first try: e transpose e over sigma squared is the sum of e i over sigma, squared. There are n terms.

19
00:04:07,951 --> 00:04:20,537
The problem: the e i are not independent. From video 14 b, the variance matrix of e is sigma squared times I minus H. This matrix is not diagonal.

20
00:04:20,987 --> 00:04:33,723
Here is I minus H for data set 1. Blue entries are positive, red entries are negative. The off-diagonal entries are not zero. So the residuals are correlated.

21
00:04:34,173 --> 00:04:44,947
The diagonal entries are less than 1. So each residual has a variance smaller than sigma squared. e i over sigma is not standard normal either.

22
00:04:45,397 --> 00:05:03,573
The smallest case shows it clearly. Take n equal to 2, with an intercept only. Then e i is y i minus y bar. The two residuals always add up to zero. Every simulated pair lies on the orange line. If you know e 1, you know e 2.

23
00:05:04,023 --> 00:05:14,113
So the first try fails. The solution is to rewrite e transpose e over sigma squared as a sum of squared normal variables that are independent.

24
00:05:14,563 --> 00:05:32,035
The tool is the eigen decomposition. I minus H is symmetric. So it can be written as gamma transpose, D, gamma. Gamma inverse equals gamma transpose. D is a diagonal matrix of the eigenvalues lambda 1 to lambda n.

25
00:05:32,485 --> 00:05:52,112
The rows of gamma are the eigenvectors of I minus H. They have length 1, and they are perpendicular to each other. In the n equals 2 case, v 1 points along the direction 1, 1, with eigenvalue 0. v 2 points along 1, minus 1, with eigenvalue 1.

26
00:05:52,562 --> 00:06:00,178
Define e tilde as gamma times e. Each entry of e tilde is e measured along one eigenvector.

27
00:06:00,628 --> 00:06:19,487
In the n equals 2 case, gamma rotates the picture by 45 degrees. e tilde 1 is e 1 plus e 2, over root 2. This is always zero. e tilde 2 is e 1 minus e 2, over root 2. All the variation is now on one axis.

28
00:06:19,937 --> 00:06:31,158
Now find the distribution of e tilde. First the mean. Gamma is constant, so it moves out. The mean of e is zero. So the mean of e tilde is zero.

29
00:06:31,608 --> 00:06:39,373
Next the variance. Use the rule: gamma on the left, the variance of e in the middle, gamma transpose on the right.

30
00:06:39,823 --> 00:06:43,706
The variance of e is sigma squared times I minus H.

31
00:06:44,156 --> 00:07:01,265
Put in the eigen decomposition. Gamma times gamma transpose is the identity, on both sides. What is left is sigma squared times D. For data set 1, D has the entries 1, 1, 0, 0, 0 on the diagonal.

32
00:07:01,715 --> 00:07:13,897
e tilde is a constant matrix times e, and e is multivariate normal. So e tilde is multivariate normal, with mean zero and variance sigma squared D.

33
00:07:14,347 --> 00:07:30,155
D is diagonal. So all covariances are zero. For the multivariate normal, this means the e tilde i are independent. Each e tilde i is normal, with mean zero and variance sigma squared lambda i.

34
00:07:30,605 --> 00:07:47,010
Why is this useful? e tilde transpose e tilde equals gamma e, transpose, gamma e. That is e transpose, gamma transpose gamma, e. Gamma transpose gamma is the identity. So the result is e transpose e.

35
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:56,505
In the picture, the rotation does not change the length of a vector. The green vector has the same length before and after the rotation.

36
00:07:56,955 --> 00:08:10,353
So e transpose e over sigma squared equals e tilde transpose e tilde over sigma squared. That is the sum of e tilde i over sigma, squared. Call each term Z i squared.

37
00:08:10,803 --> 00:08:20,829
Each Z i is e tilde i over sigma. So the Z i are independent, and Z i is normal with mean zero and variance lambda i.

38
00:08:21,279 --> 00:08:29,173
This is progress. The terms are independent now. But a chi square needs standard normal terms, with variance 1.

39
00:08:29,623 --> 00:08:45,004
So e transpose e over sigma squared is a sum of n independent squared normal variables. Two things are still missing. The terms must have variance 1. And there must be exactly n minus p plus 1 of them.

40
00:08:45,454 --> 00:08:54,585
So we will show: exactly n minus p plus 1 of the eigenvalues equal 1, and all the other eigenvalues equal 0.

41
00:08:55,035 --> 00:09:14,107
A term with lambda i equal to 0 has variance zero. So that Z i is always zero, and it adds nothing to the sum. For data set 1 the eigenvalues are 1, 1, 0, 0, 0. Two terms are left, and 2 is n minus p plus 1.

42
00:09:14,557 --> 00:09:37,298
Step 1. Show that every eigenvalue is 0 or 1. We know that I minus H times I minus H equals I minus H. A matrix with this property is idempotent. By definition, an eigenvalue lambda i with eigenvector v i satisfies lambda i v i equals I minus H, times v i.

43
00:09:37,748 --> 00:09:42,292
Start from lambda i v i equals I minus H, times v i.

44
00:09:42,742 --> 00:09:49,569
I minus H is idempotent. So we can replace it by I minus H, times I minus H.

45
00:09:50,019 --> 00:09:58,232
The right part, I minus H times v i, is lambda i v i. Lambda i is a number, so it moves to the front.

46
00:09:58,682 --> 00:10:06,063
Again, I minus H times v i is lambda i v i. So the result is lambda i squared v i.

47
00:10:06,513 --> 00:10:25,116
So lambda i v i equals lambda i squared v i. Move everything to one side: lambda i times 1 minus lambda i, times v i, is zero. An eigenvector is not the zero vector. So lambda i times 1 minus lambda i is zero.

48
00:10:25,566 --> 00:10:36,894
The picture shows the line lambda and the curve lambda squared. They meet only at 0 and at 1. So every eigenvalue of I minus H is 0 or 1.

49
00:10:37,344 --> 00:10:47,563
Step 2. Count the eigenvalues that equal 1. Each eigenvalue is 0 or 1. So the number of ones is the sum of the eigenvalues.

50
00:10:48,013 --> 00:11:08,215
The sum of the eigenvalues is the trace of D. Gamma times gamma transpose is the identity, so we can put it after D. The trace of a product does not change when we move the first factor to the end. So this is the trace of gamma transpose D gamma, which is the trace of I minus H.

51
00:11:08,665 --> 00:11:12,185
The trace of a difference is the difference of the traces.

52
00:11:12,635 --> 00:11:20,315
The trace of the n by n identity is n. H is X, X transpose X inverse, X transpose.

53
00:11:20,765 --> 00:11:37,640
Use the rule: the trace of A B equals the trace of B A. Move X from the front to the end. The product becomes X transpose X inverse, times X transpose X. This is a small matrix, p plus 1 by p plus 1.

54
00:11:38,090 --> 00:11:47,839
It is the identity of size p plus 1. Its trace is p plus 1. So the trace of I minus H is n minus p plus 1.

55
00:11:48,289 --> 00:11:54,817
So exactly n minus p plus 1 eigenvalues equal 1, and the others equal 0.

56
00:11:55,267 --> 00:12:13,998
Check this with data set 1. Here n is 5 and p is 2. The diagonal entries of I minus H are 0.15, 0.45, 0.8, 0.45 and 0.15. Their sum, the trace, is 2.

57
00:12:14,448 --> 00:12:24,475
The eigenvalues of I minus H are 1, 1, 0, 0 and 0. Each one is 0 or 1, as step 1 says. Their sum is 2.

58
00:12:24,925 --> 00:12:32,903
n minus p plus 1 is 5 minus 3, which is 2. The trace and the number of ones agree, as step 2 says.

59
00:12:33,353 --> 00:12:38,857
The trace of H is 3, which is p plus 1: one for each column of X.

60
00:12:39,307 --> 00:12:53,323
Here is the proof in three steps. One: after the rotation, e transpose e over sigma squared is a sum of n squared independent normal variables Z i, with variances lambda i.

61
00:12:53,773 --> 00:13:03,672
Two: I minus H is idempotent. So each lambda i is 0 or 1. Each Z i is standard normal, or it is always zero.

62
00:13:04,122 --> 00:13:14,874
Three: the trace shows that the eigenvalues add up to n minus p plus 1. So exactly n minus p plus 1 of the Z i are standard normal.

63
00:13:15,324 --> 00:13:30,513
So e transpose e over sigma squared is a sum of n minus p plus 1 squared independent standard normal variables. It has a chi square distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

64
00:13:30,963 --> 00:13:46,451
A simulation checks this. We use data set 1, with sigma equal to 1, and 20,000 simulated data sets. The histogram of e transpose e agrees with the chi square density with 2 degrees of freedom.

65
00:13:46,901 --> 00:14:06,443
The mean of a chi square variable is its degrees of freedom. So the expected value of e transpose e is n minus p plus 1 times sigma squared. This is why sigma squared hat divides by n minus p plus 1. It answers the question from video 14 b.

66
00:14:06,893 --> 00:14:29,485
This completes the result of slide 44. Beta j hat minus beta j, over the square root of sigma squared hat V j j, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. Video 16 uses it for tests and confidence intervals. Now four short questions. Pause the video and try them.

67
00:14:29,935 --> 00:14:38,831
Why are the residuals not independent? Their variance matrix is sigma squared times I minus H, which is not diagonal.

68
00:14:39,281 --> 00:14:52,763
Why are sigma squared hat and beta hat independent? Beta hat and e are jointly multivariate normal, with a zero covariance block. Sigma squared hat is a function of e only.

69
00:14:53,213 --> 00:15:01,427
Why are the eigenvalues 0 or 1? I minus H is idempotent, so lambda v equals lambda squared v.

70
00:15:01,877 --> 00:15:11,306
Why n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom? The trace of I minus H is n minus the trace of the p plus 1 identity.

71
00:15:11,756 --> 00:15:22,785
To recap. The t test for one coefficient needs three facts. Beta hat and e are multivariate normal with zero covariance, so they are independent.

72
00:15:23,235 --> 00:15:39,534
For the chi square result, we rotate e with the eigenvectors of I minus H. The length does not change, and the new entries are independent. Idempotent means each eigenvalue is 0 or 1, and the trace counts the ones.

73
00:15:39,984 --> 00:15:52,784
The mean of the chi square gives the expected sum of squared residuals. So sigma squared hat is unbiased. Video 16 uses these results for inference on each coefficient.
